The change belongs in the training pipeline's request check. `TrainingPipeline.request_entries` (`reef/train/pipeline.py:468-491`) loads every named sample with one `self.store.get(request.samples)` call and builds a full list of `(stored, sample)` entries. `TrainingPipeline.check_request` (`pipeline.py:493-504`) calls `request_entries` after a second `store.get` for the unknown-id check, so the whole request is held twice. `Trainer.check_request` (`reef/train/trainer.py:493-496`) only forwards to the pipeline under the lock, so it needs no logic change. The store read that loads all samples at once is in `reef/storage/samples.py` (`TrainerSamples`, lines 222-293), and the batching would need to go there too.

I did not write the change or run tests. I confirmed `request_entries` and `check_request` by reading the code. I did not open `samples.py` or `trainer.py`'s queued-request path, so those two are placed by the locate results and the call chain, not by reading their bodies.

{"files": ["reef/train/pipeline.py", "reef/train/trainer.py", "reef/storage/samples.py"]}

The change belongs in the serving worker's stop branch. `reef/dispatcher/serving.py` keeps the `activated` map (line 98), and the `STOP_SERVING_KIND` branch (lines 191-201) calls `runtime.stop_serving(served)` but never clears the entry. The fix is to drop `self.activated[item.scenario]` there when it matches the stopped artifact. That is the file that must change.

I did not write the change. The other two paths are supporting context: `reef/inference/serving.py` holds `stop_serving` (line 358), which the worker calls, and `reef/storage/served_models.py` defines the stop kind and payload.

{"files": ["reef/dispatcher/serving.py", "reef/inference/serving.py", "reef/storage/served_models.py"]}

The bug is in `EvaluationResults.settle` (`reef/service/artifact_evaluations.py:208-233`). It reads the rollouts at line 210, before it takes the scenario lock at line 211. A retry can queue new rollouts in that window. The stale read then makes the thread see old failed items and end the evaluation as failed. The fix is to move the `self.rollouts(...)` call inside the `locked_scenario` block. I did not write the change.

The other candidates from `locate` (`rollouts.py`, `workers.py`, `dispatcher.py`, `registry.py`) are not involved. The lock and the read are both in `settle`, so this one file is enough.

{"files": ["reef/service/artifact_evaluations.py"]}

The names in your change don't match this repo. `rollout_calls`, `StoredCalls`, and `attempt_trajectories` don't exist anywhere outside tests. The closest code is the trajectory path: `make_trajectory` in `reef/core/trajectories.py` reads all records and builds the trajectory. It's called from `reef/record2sample/trajectories.py`, which passes it `record_set.calls`. The linking by message hash lives in `reef/core/trajectory_index.py`. The store read API that would supply call bodies one at a time is `reef/storage/records.py`.

I did not confirm that this is the right place for the change. The `include_records=False` option and the per-call reads would have to be added, and I haven't checked whether the stored-record reads are where the memory goes. The four files below are my best guess from the code, not a verified mapping.

